Finally they add that everything will turn out more lightly if religious rites have been performed. But if nothing happens outside fate, nothing can be lightened by a religious rite. Homer means this when he presents Jupiter lamenting that he could not rescue his son Sarpedon from death against fate. The same is meant by that Greek verse to this effect:
What is prepared to be overpowers even highest Jupiter.
The whole notion of fate seems to me rightly mocked even in a line of Atellan farce;
